    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d85">

                <pb facs="0207" n="191" sameAs="frus:frus1949v06/pg191"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.2383/1–2549</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Secretary of
                            State to the Counselor</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bohlen</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1949-01-28">January 28, 1949</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I see no political objection to having some of Admiral Conolly’s staff visit GHQ
                    Fayid in Egypt for three or four weeks. They should <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >not</hi> be detailed or assigned in any way, nor should they be <hi
                        rendition="#italic">attached</hi> to this British staff. Handled as a
                    routine visit, I see no political objection to their working with the British
                    staff in an advisory or consultative role.</p>
                <p>I agree with Satterthwaite’s views in the event the visit is to be prolonged.
                    Please have appropriate action taken in accordance with the above.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>D[<hi rendition="#small-caps">ean</hi>] A[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >cheson</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
